@startuml

salt
{
  <b>ORE STUDIO - REPORTING: ANALYTICS - Group Consolidated Exposure - ACMCOR - risk</b>
  --
  <b>THE CONFIGURATION THIS DEFINITION BINDS</b>
  --
  {#
    Configuration type | Configuration | Shared
    conventions | ORE conventions 2026 | no
    pricing_engines | ACME pricing engines | no
    todays_market | London close 2026-10-16 | no
    curve_configuration | ACME curve set | no
    risk_report_config | Group risk configuration | yes, by 4 definitions
  }
  --
  <b>ANALYTICS OF Group risk configuration: on and off, in display order</b>
  --
  {#
    On | Analytic | Order | Parameters
    Y | npv | 1 | -
    Y | sensitivity | 2 | shift_size = 0.0001, shift_type = Absolute
    N | cashflow | 3 | -
    Y | portfolioDetails | 4 | -
    Y | pfe | 5 | horizon = 10, quantile = 0.95
    N | simulation | 6 | -
  }
  A row that is off stays on the configuration and reads as off. An analytic is turned off by setting active to
  false and never by deleting the row, so the history shows the report used to produce it. No control on this
  panel removes a row.
  --
  <b>THE ONE RULE BETWEEN ANALYTICS</b>
  --
  pfe reads a cube that simulation writes, so pfe on without simulation cannot work.
  <b>REFUSED: pfe is on and simulation is off.</b> The rule lives in the ACME seeder, in
  generate_risk_config_data.py, check_exposure_needs_simulation, and in no service. The server accepts this
  set, so this panel states the rule itself.
  --
  <b>CHANGE A PARAMETER</b>
  --
  {#
    Parameter | Value
    sensitivity.shift_size | 0.00005
    sensitivity.shift_type | Absolute
    pfe.horizon | 10
  }
  A parameter change writes one row: its parameter_name and its value.
  --
  <b>REVIEW: EXACTLY WHAT WILL BE WRITTEN</b>
  --
  {#
    Write | Analytic | Field | Was | Will be
    configuration_analytic | cashflow | active | Y | N
    configuration_analytic | saccr | active | - | Y
    configuration_analytic_parameter | sensitivity | shift_size | 0.0001 | 0.00005
  }
  Confirm: write 2 analytic rows and 1 parameter row to Group risk configuration?
  {
    [ Cancel ] | [ Write ]
  }
  No row is deleted. No run is started, and no schedule, binding or processing phase is touched.
  --
  <b>WHAT THE CHANGE DOES NOT REACH</b>
  --
  Past runs keep the configuration they ran with, with their outputs and their errors. The configuration is
  shared, so every definition bound to it runs the new set on its next run, including the three definitions the
  person did not open.
  --
  <b>RUNS THE CHANGE MAKES INCOMPARABLE</b>
  --
  {#
    Run | Started | Configuration it ran with
    run 41c9f0a7 | 2026-10-16 06:04 | inference from start time
    run 5b02e6d3 | 2026-10-15 06:03 | inference from start time
  }
  The results screen compares a run with a previous run of the same definition. A run after this change did not
  produce the same measures, so a comparison between it and a run above is not a comparison. The screen marks a
  comparison partial where a side carried errors, and it cannot mark this one: nothing says which configuration
  version a run used.
  --
  <b>AFTER THE WRITE: READ BACK AND THE DEFINITION'S VERSIONS</b>
  --
  {#
    Analytic | active | Order
    cashflow | N | 3
    saccr | Y | 7
  }
  {#
    Definition version | Stated
    v7 | 2026-10-17, the version this write records
    v6 | 2026-09-30
  }
  The panel states the stored values, not the values the person typed.
  --
  <b>WHAT THIS PANEL CANNOT SHOW</b>
  --
  Which configuration version a run used: not readable. A configuration's own version history: not readable.
  Whether pfe without simulation is refused: it is not, by any service. The screen states these absences rather
  than drawing a validation it does not have.
}

@enduml
